<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nijmegen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nimwegen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nijmegen is a city in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a>, and claims to be the oldest city in the country. It grew from the Batavian settlement and Roman town of Ulpia Noviomagus, became an imperial residence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and later a city of the Duchy of Guelders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaties of Nijmegen</a> of 1678–1679 were signed there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nijmegen.jpg" alt="Nijmegen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Veringmeier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_Nijmegen._Gelderland._Netherlands.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nijmegen lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a> close to the German border, on hills that give a wide view over the Waal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> valued the position and built a military camp there in the first century BCE. The city is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a> and has been a university city since 1923, when a Catholic university, now Radboud University Nijmegen, opened there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By 69 CE a village called Oppidum Batavorum had formed near the camp. It was destroyed when the Batavi, the original inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> delta, revolted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> then built a larger camp for Legio X Gemina. In 98 Nijmegen was the first of two settlements in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to receive Roman city rights, and in 104 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> renamed the town Ulpia Noviomagus Batavorum.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> kept a palace there in 777. Henry VI, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, was born there in 1165, and in 1230 his son Frederick II granted the town city rights. In 1247 it was ceded to the count of Guelders as security for a loan that was never repaid, so it has remained part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbourg">Limbourg</a> brothers, painters, were born and educated in the city. It hosted the negotiations that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678, which did not bring a lasting peace. Its fortifications were dismantled in 1874.</p><p>On 22 February 1944 American aircraft bombed the centre, killing over 750 people; the NIOD later concluded that the raid was an accident caused by poor communications. In September 1944, during Operation Market Garden, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> fought to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a> bridges, and British Grenadier Guards and elements of the American 82nd Airborne Division liberated the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a> was first bridged by a rail bridge in 1878 and by a road bridge in 1936. The Smetius Collection, assembled by Johannes Smetius in the 17th century, preserves Roman finds from the town, and the city is known for the Vierdaagse, the International Four Days Marches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nijmegen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their noble families commanded these units, and their chief town Batavodurum stood at <b>Nijmegen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/batavi">Batavi</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nijmegen</b>, Roermond, Arnhem and Zutphen became the four quarters of the territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Duchy of Guelders</a></li></ul></section>
